L57: arXiv:2511.14719v1 [cs.CV] 18 Nov 2025
L58: # Zero-shot Synthetic Video Realism Enhancement via Structure-aware Denoising
L59: Yifan Wang^{*}^{*} * *Equal contribution. Affiliation: The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology    Liya Ji^{†}^{†}footnotemark: Affiliation: The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology    Zhanghan Ke Affiliation: The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology    Harry Yang Affiliation: The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology Affiliation: Everlyn AI    Sernam Lim Affiliation: Everlyn AI Affiliation: University of Central Florida    Qifeng Chen^{2}^{2} 2 Corresponding author.
L60: Affiliation: The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology
L61: ###### Abstract
L62: We propose an approach to enhancing synthetic video realism, which can re-render synthetic videos from a simulator in photorealistic fashion. Our realism enhancement approach is a zero-shot framework that focuses on preserving the multi-level structures from synthetic videos into the enhanced one in both spatial and temporal domains, built upon a diffusion video foundational model without further fine-tuning.
L63: Specifically, we incorporate an effective modification to have the generation/denoising process conditioned on estimated structure-aware information from the synthetic video, such as depth maps, semantic maps, and edge maps, by an auxiliary model, rather than extracting the information from a simulator. This guidance ensures that the enhanced videos are consistent with the original synthetic video at both the structural and semantic levels.
L64: Our approach is a simple yet general and powerful approach to enhancing synthetic video realism: we show that our approach outperforms existing baselines in structural consistency with the original video while maintaining state-of-the-art photorealism quality in our experiments.
L65: ^{3}^{3}footnotetext: Project page: cite46†https://wyf0824.github.io/Video_Realism_Enhancement/†wyf0824.github.io L66: ## 1 Introduction
L67: Training models for autonomous vehicles requires vast amounts of data, particularly for rare long-tail scenarios. However, the collection and labeling of real-world video data are costly and often fail to capture these critical events.
L68: To address this challenge, researchers have turned to synthetic data generated from simulators of the environment (cite47†Agarwal et al., 2025 ), which offers a scalable and controllable means of producing the data required to accelerate research and development in autonomous vehicles (cite48†Hu et al., 2023 ; cite49†Zhou et al., 2025 ). A key challenge here is the domain gap between synthetic and real-world data that can introduce biases that adversely affect model performance.
L69: To alleviate this issue, we focus on enhancing the realism of synthetic videos in this work.
L70: Initial efforts to tackle this issue used video-to-video translation techniques (cite50†Wang et al., 2018 ; cite51†Zhuo et al., 2022 ), which converted simulated driving sequences (e.g., GTA V (cite52†Richter et al., 2016 )) into realistic counterparts using GAN-based methods (cite53†Goodfellow et al., 2014 ).
L71: These prior works demonstrated the feasibility of rendering more realistic driving footage, but they often suffered from limitations such as low resolution, poor temporal consistency, and semantic inconsistency with the original synthetic video.
L72: Recent advancements in diffusion models (cite54†Rombach et al., 2022 ; cite55†Peebles and Xie, 2023 ) and the availability of large-scale datasets (cite56†Caesar et al., 2020 ; cite57†Yang et al., 2024a ) have significantly enhanced generative fidelity, yielding sharper, high-resolution, and more realistic outputs.
L108: ControlNet is another fine-tuning control method that generates high-fidelity results by conditioning different inputs, such as Canny, depth, or segmentation maps. Cosmos-transfer (cite75†Alhaija et al., 2025 ) is a controllable version based on the world foundation model, Cosmos-predict (cite47†Agarwal et al., 2025 ), with the DiT structure using the ControlNet technique. VACE (cite85†Jiang et al. (2025) ) is the controllable version of WAN (cite81†Wan et al. (2025) ) via a context adapter structure.
L109: In this paper, we combine inversion techniques with ControlNet to enhance the realism of synthetic videos through structure-aware denoising.
L110: ## 3 Method
L111: 
L112: cite86†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Overview of our pipeline on synthetic video realism enhancement. We inject DDIM inversion into the ControlNet version of the world model (cite47†Agarwal et al., 2025 ) in a zero-shot structure-aware denoising manner, aiming to improve structural consistency while maintaining photorealism.
L113: The foundation of our proposed framework is Cosmos-Transfer1 (cite75†Alhaija et al., 2025 ), a state-of-the-art model that addresses the synthetic-to-real domain transfer problem in video generation. Its architecture is distinguished by the integration of a Video ControlNet, which enables robust temporal and spatial conditioning.
L114: ### 3.1 Preliminary: Cosmos-transfer
L115: #### Multi-modal Conditioning
L116: A key feature of Cosmos-Transfer1 is its ability to simultaneously process multiple spatial conditions. The model accepts a text prompt for semantic guidance, along with a set of three distinct spatial control maps: a depth map (cite87†Yang et al., 2024b ), a segmentation map (cite88†Ravi et al., 2024 ), and a Canny edge map. The text prompt is converted into an embedding $\mathbf{c}_{\text{text}}$ by a text encoder, while the spatial maps are collectively denoted as $\mathbf{c}_{\text{spatial}}$.
L117: #### Structure-aware Denoising Process
L118: The core of our generative process is a DiT-based denoising network (cite55†Peebles and Xie, 2023 ), $\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}$, which operates in a continuous time framework. The network is trained to predict the noise component $\bm{\epsilon}$ from a noisy image $\mathbf{x}_{t}$. Given $\mathbf{x}_{t}$ at noise level $\sigma_{t}$ and the conditions $\mathbf{c}_{\text{spatial}}$ and $\mathbf{c}_{\text{text}}$, the predicted noise at timestep t, which we denote as $\mathbf{n}_{t}$, is given by:
L119:  | $$\mathbf{n}_{t}=\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},\sigma_{t},\mathbf{c}_{\text{spatial}},\mathbf{c}_{\text{text}}).$$  |  | (1)
L120: 
L121: To integrate the spatial conditions $\mathbf{c}_{\text{spatial}}$, we employ a ControlNet structure that runs in parallel to the main DiT backbone. The control signals are injected into the transformer blocks, with their influence governed by an indicator function. For each block $i$, the final output feature map, $\mathbf{h}_{i}^{\text{final}}$, is computed as:
L122:  | $$\mathbf{h}_{i}^{\text{final}}=\mathbf{h}_{i}^{\text{main}}+\mathbb{I}(i\in\{1,2,3\})\cdot w_{c}\cdot\mathbf{h}_{i}^{\text{control}},$$  |  | (2)
L123: 
L124: where $\mathbb{I}(\cdot)$ is the indicator function that is equal to 1 if the condition is true and 0 otherwise. The term $w_{c}$ is the control weight. Note that we balance the influence of the spatial conditions only for the first three blocks.
L125: #### Image Sampling with Classifier-Free Guidance
L126: During inference, Classifier-Free Guidance (CFG) (cite89†Ho and Salimans, 2022 ) is utilized to enhance adherence to the text prompt while steering the generation away from the negative prompt. At each timestep $t$, two noise predictions: a conditional one, $\mathbf{n}_{t,\text{cond}}$, using the target text prompt, and an unconditional one, $\mathbf{n}_{t,\text{uncond}}$, using a negative prompt are generated.
L127: Following the EDM (cite90†Karras et al., 2022 ) formulation, these noise predictions are converted into corresponding estimated clean images, $\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0,t,m\in\text{\lx@text@lbrace cond, uncond\lx@text@rbrace}}$, using:
L128:  | $$\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0,t,m}=\left(\frac{\sigma_{\text{data}}^{2}}{\sigma_{t}^{2}+\sigma_{\text{data}}^{2}}\right)\mathbf{x}_{t,m}+\left(\frac{\sigma_{t}\sigma_{\text{data}}}{\sqrt{\sigma_{t}^{2}+\sigma_{\text{data}}^{2}}}\right)\mathbf{n}_{t,m},$$  |  | (3)
L129: where $\sigma_{t}$ is the noise level in the timestep $t$, and $\sigma_{\text{data}}$ is the standard deviation of the training data. These two estimates are then combined into a single guided estimate, $\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0,t,\text{cfg}}$, by extrapolating from the unconditional towards the conditional prediction:
L130: 
L131:  | $$\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0,t,\text{cfg}}=\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0,t,\text{cond}}+w_{\text{cfg}}\cdot(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0,t,\text{cond}}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0,t,\text{uncond}}),$$  |  | (4)
L132: where $w_{\text{cfg}}$ denotes CFG. $\mathbf{x}_{t-1}$ is then computed within an Euler sampler, as:
L133: 
L134:  | $$\mathbf{x}_{t-1}=\mathbf{x}_{t}+\left(\frac{\mathbf{x}_{t}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0,t,\text{cfg}}}{\sigma_{t}}\right)(\sigma_{t-1}-\sigma_{t}).$$  |  | (5)
L135: ### 3.2 Synthetic Video Realism Enhancement
L136: To bridge the synthetic-to-real gap, we introduce a novel pipeline that enhances photorealism while preserving the low-frequency structural information of a source simulation. While traditional methods might directly use high-frequency conditioning maps (e.g., Canny edges, segmentation) from a synthetic image/video to guide a denoising process from pure noise, we found this often fails to retain the global composition.
L137: Our method, in contrast, employs a latent inversion and subsequent re-generation process to achieve a more faithful transfer.
L138: #### Source Simulation and Conditioning:
L139: Given a synthetic video, we first generate the video caption using a model such as Videollama3 (cite91†Zhang et al., 2025 ). This model concurrently provides two text prompts: an inversion prompt ($\mathbf{c}_{\text{text}}^{\text{inv}}$), which accurately describes the synthetic video content, and a positive prompt ($\mathbf{c}_{\text{text}}^{\text{real}}$), designed to elicit photorealism. We extract the corresponding spatial conditioning maps $\mathbf{c}_{\text{spatial}}$ (depth, segmentation, Canny).
L140: #### Deterministic Latent Inversion:
L141: 
L142: The core of our contribution is to find a unique noise latent $\mathbf{x}_{T}$ that deterministically reproduces the synthetic video $\mathbf{x}_{0}^{\text{sim}}$. This is achieved by reversing the generative process through a DDIM-style inversion. Starting with $\mathbf{x}_{0}=\mathbf{x}_{0}^{\text{sim}}$, we iteratively add noise by reversing the Euler sampler step for $t=0,\dots,T-1$, where $\sigma_{t+1}$ is the noise level of the next inversion step:
L143:  | $$\mathbf{x}_{t+1}=\mathbf{x}_{t}+\left(\frac{\mathbf{x}_{t}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0,t,\text{inv}}}{\sigma_{t}}\right)(\sigma_{t+1}-\sigma_{t}).$$  |  | (6)
L144: At each inversion step, the guided clean video latent estimate $\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{0,t,\text{inv}}$ is computed using the inversion prompt $\mathbf{c}_{\text{text}}^{\text{inv}}$ and the spatial maps $\mathbf{c}_{\text{spatial}}$. This computation follows the full generative logic: the ControlNet-integrated denoising network first predicts the noise (Eq. cite92†1 , cite93†2 ), which is then used to derive the clean estimate via the EDM formulation (Eq. cite94†3 ).
L145: This process effectively encodes the full structural and low-frequency information of $\mathbf{x}_{0}^{\text{sim}}$ into the final latent $\mathbf{x}_{T}$.
L146: #### Structure-aware denoising for Photorealism:
L147: With the information-rich latent $\mathbf{x}_{T}$ secured, we perform the final generative step. We initiate the standard denoising process, as detailed in Section cite13†3.1 , starting from the inverted latent $\mathbf{x}_{T}$. However, for this forward process, we use the positive prompt $\mathbf{c}_{\text{text}}^{\text{real}}$ generated in the initial step. The spatial conditions $\mathbf{c}_{\text{spatial}}$ remain unchanged to ensure strict structural consistency with the original simulation.
L148: This two-stage approach allows Cosmos-transfer1 to leverage the inverted latent to preserve content and structure while using the new positive prompt to steer the synthesis into a realistic domain, effectively bridging the synthetic-to-real gap.
L151: To evaluate our framework, we built a custom benchmark using the CARLA simulator (cite95†Dosovitskiy et al., 2017b ; cite96†Sun et al., 2022 ). The benchmark consists of 900 unique video sequences, each 121 frames long, designed to rigorously test the synthetic-to-real generalization of our method.
L152: The sequences were generated under a diverse matrix of environmental conditions, systematically combining different times of day (daytime and nighttime) with various weather scenarios (clear/sunny, rainy, and dense fog). This comprehensive dataset serves as our primary testbed for performance assessment.
L153: ### 4.2 Evaluation methods
L154: 
L155: cite97†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: Qualitative results of our methods on synthetic video realism enhancement. We could maintain the photorealism and improve the structural consistency in the diverse outdoor conditions.
L230: Table 3: Ablation study on multi-conditions and control methods. Our method utilizes conditions, including semantic maps, Canny edges, and depth maps.
L232: We analyze the key components in our framework: the multi-condition ControlNet and the DDIM inversion process. We conducted an ablation study, with results presented in Table cite111†3 , to understand how each part affects video quality. When we add blurred video as an additional condition, it achieves the best alignment scores. However, its photorealism score collapses to a mere 19.2%. This is because the blurred video contains the original video’s style, which decreases the video’s photorealism.
