Kandinsky 5.0: A Family of Foundation Models for Image and Video Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.14993v1)
citeturn6496view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({"ref_id":"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.14993v1","pattern":"architecture"}); Total lines: 2217
L281: Both models utilized an autoregressive architecture to generate 256$\times$256 resolution images.
L822: #### Advanced Domain Organization and SFT-soup Composition Ablation Study
L823: 
L824: For the Text-to-Image (T2I) model, we employed a more sophisticated hierarchical domain organization strategy.
L825: 
L826: We experimented with multiple domain decomposition strategies for following optimal SFT-soup composition:
L827: 
L828:   * •
L829: 
L830: VLM Domain Classification: Full fine-tuning was performed on each VLM domain from the previous section with reduced batch size (64 vs. 4096 in pretrain) and learning rate (1e-5 vs. 1e-4).
L831: 
L832:   * •
L833: CLIP Embedding Clustering: We alternativeively used CLIP-ViT-H-14-quickgelu embeddings with k-means clustering into 9 clusters. This approach increased data diversity within individual components, reducing overfitting and enabling longer training for better realism.
L834: 
L835:   * •
L836: Hierarchical VLM Domains: Each VLM domain was further divided into 2-9 semantically coherent subdomains. Separate full fine-tuning on these smaller, more homogeneous subsets allowed for targeted improvement in specific visual characteristics while maintaining overall coherence.
L837: For weight merging in SFT-soup composition, we compared three approaches: equal weights (1/N), weights proportional to dataset size, and weights proportional to the square root of dataset size. Our experiments showed that equal weights or root-proportional weights performed best. The hierarchical domain approach yielded the best results in side-by-side evaluations, achieving high realism, improved text rendering quality, and compositionally correct generated images.
L838: The resulting SFT dataset represents a carefully curated collection of visual content that exemplifies high aesthetics and enables robust model fine-tuning across diverse visual domains.
L839: ## 5  Kandinsky 5.0 Architecture
L840: ### 5.1  Model Overview
L841: All models in the Kandinsky 5.0 family are built upon a unified architecture based on a latent diffusion pipeline cite98†Rombach et al. (2022) and trained using the Flow Matching paradigm cite97†Lipman et al. (2023) . The core of this architecture is a specially designed Diffusion Transformer with cross-attention (CrossDiT) for multimodal fusion of visual and textual information. The number of CrossDiT blocks varies depending on the model size.
L842: The architecture also integrates text and visual encoders for efficient input processing:
L843:   * •
L844: 
L845: Text Encoding: Text representations are extracted using the Qwen2.5-VL model cite186†Bai et al. (2025) , a transformer decoder architecture that generates rich text embeddings. These embeddings are further processed by a Linguistic Token Refiner module before being passed into the main CrossDiT backbone.
L846: 
L847:   * •
L848: Visual Encoding: We use FLUX.1-dev VAE^{18}^{18} 18 cite218†https://huggingface.co/black-forest-labs/FLUX.1-dev†huggingface.co cite103†Labs (2024) to encode images. Video latents are obtained using an encoder from the HunyuanVideo VAE cite120†Kong et al. (2025) , which produces compact latent representations suitable for the diffusion process while maintaining temporal consistency.
L849: Below, we examine in more detail the structure of the CrossDiT backbone architecture (Section cite17†5.2 ), the CrossDiT blocks (Section cite19†5.3 ), and our proposed Neighborhood Adaptive Block-Level Attention (NABLA) mechanism cite131†Mikhailov et al. (2025) , which enables significant acceleration and optimization for video generation tasks (Section cite20†5.4 ). In our work, we place strong emphasis on computational efficiency and training stability, which are discussed further in Section cite47†7 .
L850: ### 5.2  Diffusion Transformer (CrossDiT) Architecture
L851: 
L852: cite219†Image: Refer to caption Figure 10: CrossDiT architecture.
L853: 
L854: The CrossDiT architecture is illustrated in Figure cite220†10 . Its core consists of a sequence of CrossDiT blocks. The model takes four distinct types of inputs:
L855: 
L856:   * •
L857: Text: Text embeddings from Qwen2.5-VL are passed through a Linear layer and LayerNorm cite221†Ba et al. (2016) . One-dimensional positional embeddings are also generated for the text using Rotary Position Encoding (RoPE) cite222†Su et al. (2023) . These are combined and fed into the Linguistic Token Refiner (LTF) module.
L858: This module, which is a CrossDiT block without the cross-attention component (see Figure cite223†11 ), serves to enhance the text representation and eliminate the positional bias inherited from the pre-trained text encoder cite224†Ma et al. (2025a) . The refined text queries are then fed into the main CrossDiT blocks via the cross-attention mechanism cite225†Lin et al. (2021) .
L859:   * •
L860: 
L861: Time: The diffusion timestep value is processed by a block comprising Sinusoidal Encoding (SE) cite226†Vaswani et al. (2023) and a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP).
L862: 
L863:   * •
L864: CLIP Text Embedding: A single text embedding of the full video description from CLIP ViT-L/14 model^{19}^{19} 19 cite227†https://huggingface.co/sentence-transformers/clip-ViT-L-14†huggingface.co cite154†Radford et al. (2021) is passed through a linear layer and LayerNorm, and then summed element-wise with the time embedding. This resulting sum, along with the output from the final CrossDiT block, is fed into the Adaptive Normalization Layer cite111†Peebles and Xie (2023) .
L865: 
L866:   * •
L867: Visual: Image latents from FLUX.1-dev VAE or video latents from the HunyuanVideo VAE encoder are used to generate 3D Rotary Positional Embeddings, which are then fed into every CrossDiT block.
L868: ##### Architecture hyperparameters.
L869: 
L870: For all models, we use Qwen2.5-VL as the main text encoder with 7 billion parameters, an embedding size of 3584 and a maximum context length of 256. We also use CLIP ViT-L/14 with a text embedding size of 768 and a maximum context length of 77. Hyperparameters for the architecture of the main CrossDiT part of our models are shown in Table cite228†3 .
L871: Table 3: CrossDiT hyperparameters for the Kandinsky 5.0 family of models.
L872: Model  | Number of  | Number of  | Linear  | Model  | Time
L873: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---
L874:  | CrossDiT  | LTF  | layer  | embedding  | embedding
L875: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---
L876:  | blocks  | blocks  | dimension  | dimension  | dimension
L877: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---
